select m.id, 3, $L$Asserting on JSON Responses$L$, $L$25 minutes$L$, $L$Reading JSON is passive; asserting on it is testing. In this hands-on lesson you write real Postman tests that check status, values and types in a JSON response.$L$, array[$L$Write a Postman test that asserts a JSON field value$L$, $L$Assert on a nested field and its data type$L$, $L$Explain why a specific assertion beats a vague one$L$], $L$## The three things worth asserting
1. **Status** — did the request succeed? `pm.response.to.have.status(200)`
2. **Value** — is a field what you expect? `expect(body.id).to.eql(7)`
3. **Type** — is it the right kind of value? `expect(body.id).to.be.a('number')`

## Writing tests in Postman
Postman's **Tests** tab runs JavaScript after the response arrives. Parse the body once, then assert:
```
const body = pm.response.json();
pm.test('id is 7 and a number', () => {
  pm.expect(body.id).to.eql(7);
  pm.expect(body.id).to.be.a('number');
});
```

## Nested and array assertions
Use the paths from Lesson 2: `body.user.orders[0].id`. Assert on what actually matters — the values a real user or downstream system depends on.

## Specific beats vague
`expect(body).to.exist` passes on almost anything. `expect(body.status).to.eql('active')` fails the moment the value is wrong. A good assertion is a **narrow** one.$L$, $L$For `GET https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users/1`, the Tests tab: `const b = pm.response.json();` then `pm.test('user 1', () => { pm.expect(pm.response.code).to.eql(200); pm.expect(b.id).to.eql(1); pm.expect(b.name).to.be.a('string'); pm.expect(b.address.city).to.exist; });`. This checks status, a value, a type and a nested field in one test.$L$, $L$- Asserting only `to.exist` when you could assert the exact value
- Calling `pm.response.json()` many times instead of parsing once into a variable
- Checking the value but never the type, so a `"7"` string slips past$L$, $L$Write assertions a future teammate can read. `pm.test('order total includes VAT', ...)` documents intent far better than `pm.test('test1', ...)`.$L$, $L$In Postman, send `GET https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users/1` and add a test that asserts the status is 200, `id` equals 1, and `name` is a string. Confirm all assertions pass.$L$, $L$Why is asserting an exact value more useful than asserting only that a field exists?$L$, $L$In a Postman test, which method parses the response body into a JSON object? (Answer: pm.response.json())$L$, array[$L$I wrote a Postman test that asserts a field value$L$, $L$I asserted on a nested field and a data type$L$, $L$All my assertions passed against a live response$L$]
